OBSERVED=json.loads('{"history_review_complete": true, "duplicate": false, "title": "Skylab: NASA\'s Space Station Broke Before Its Crew Arrived", "description": "Launched on May 14, 1973, America\'s first space station reached orbit wounded: its micrometeoroid and thermal shield was torn away, one solar array wing was lost, the other jammed. With temperatures climbing and power failing, NASA held back the first crew and raced to invent a repair. This is the story of two separate rescues \\u2014 cooling the workshop with an improvised parasol, then freeing the trapped solar array in a first-of-its-kind repair spacewalk by Pete Conrad and Joe Kerwin \\u2014 told alongside the engineers, technicians and controllers on the ground who made it possible.", "research": [{"url": "https://www.nasa.gov/history/50-years-ago-skylab-2-the-we-fix-anything-crew/", "summary": "NASA history: Skylab launched May 14, 1973; lost micrometeoroid/thermal shield; one solar wing torn off, other jammed; internal temperatures rose; crew launch delayed twice to May 25; engineers devised attitude plans, three sunshade options; Jack Kinzler designed the parasol (22x24 ft nylon/Mylar/aluminum, fishing rods, scientific airlock deployment); crew launched May 25, inspected and docked."}, {"url": "https://www.nasa.gov/history/50-years-ago-skylab-2-astronauts-deploy-jammed-solar-array-during-spacewalk/", "summary": "NASA history: after May 25 fly-around inspection confirmed damage, a standup EVA attempt to free the jammed array failed; parasol deployed May 26 lowered internal temperatures; Schweickart\'s tiger team used Neutral Buoyancy Simulator; June 7, 1973 Conrad and Kerwin cut the metal strap with a 25-foot pole and cutter and pulled the beam to full 90-degree deployment; 3 hours 25 minutes, longest Earth-orbital EVA then."}, {"url": "https://www.nasa.gov/history/50-years-ago-skylab-2-astronauts-splash-down-after-record-breaking-28-day-mission-2/", "summary": "NASA history: Skylab 2 crew splashed down June 22, 1973, recovered by USS Ticonderoga after 28 days; completed virtually all planned science; repairs enabled later 59- and 84-day flights; unflown August 1973 rescue capability noted separately; ATM first solar flare June 15; power restored allowed experiments."}], "claims": [{"text": "During its May 14, 1973 launch, Skylab lost its micrometeoroid shield, which also served as thermal protection; debris jammed one solar array wing and the other was torn away.", "source_urls": ["https://www.nasa.gov/history/50-years-ago-skylab-2-the-we-fix-anything-crew/"]}, {"text": "With temperatures rising inside the unprotected station, managers delayed the first crew\'s launch by ten days to devise repairs for the overheating and underpowered station.", "source_urls": ["https://www.nasa.gov/history/50-years-ago-skylab-2-the-we-fix-anything-crew/", "https://www.nasa.gov/history/50-years-ago-skylab-2-astronauts-deploy-jammed-solar-array-during-spacewalk/"]}, {"text": "Jack Kinzler designed a parasol sunshade of nylon, Mylar and aluminum on spring-loaded fishing rods, deployed through a scientific airlock.", "source_urls": ["https://www.nasa.gov/history/50-years-ago-skylab-2-the-we-fix-anything-crew/"]}, {"text": "After a May 25 fly-around inspection, a standup spacewalk attempt to free the jammed array failed; the parasol was deployed the next day and lowered internal temperatures.", "source_urls": ["https://www.nasa.gov/history/50-years-ago-skylab-2-astronauts-deploy-jammed-solar-array-during-spacewalk/"]}, {"text": "On June 7, 1973, Conrad and Kerwin used a 25-foot pole with a cutter to sever the strap, then pulled the array beam to full deployment; the spacewalk lasted 3 hours 25 minutes.", "source_urls": ["https://www.nasa.gov/history/50-years-ago-skylab-2-astronauts-deploy-jammed-solar-array-during-spacewalk/"]}, {"text": "Backup commander Russell Schweickart led a tiger team that recreated the jammed-array problem in the Neutral Buoyancy Simulator to develop procedures.", "source_urls": ["https://www.nasa.gov/history/50-years-ago-skylab-2-astronauts-deploy-jammed-solar-array-during-spacewalk/"]}, {"text": "Skylab 2 splashed down June 22, 1973, recovered by USS Ticonderoga, completing virtually all planned science objectives and enabling later 59- and 84-day missions.", "source_urls": ["https://www.nasa.gov/history/50-years-ago-skylab-2-astronauts-splash-down-after-record-breaking-28-day-mission-2/"]}, {"text": "An inflight rescue capability existed for Skylab crews but was never needed during any of the three missions.", "source_urls": ["https://www.nasa.gov/history/50-years-ago-skylab-2-astronauts-splash-down-after-record-breaking-28-day-mission-2/"]}], "assets": [{"type": "image", "role": "context", "label": "Skylab on Pad 39A and its Saturn IB crew rocket, May 1973", "media_ids": ["M21ca833754a64343"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "event", "label": "Skylab 1 launch on Saturn V, May 14, 1973", "media_ids": ["M06f8ec6bf59878b4"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "context", "label": "Orbital Workshop solar array wing during ground deployment test, 1970", "media_ids": ["M1a78e8db95edb7db"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "event", "label": "Skylab 2 crew portrait: Kerwin, Conrad, Weitz, May 1973", "media_ids": ["M80233ab86f9e8d48"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "event", "label": "Skylab in orbit with visible launch damage, February 1974 view", "media_ids": ["M34eb190abd85c461"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "context", "label": "Skylab 2 crew breakfast before launch, May 25, 1973", "media_ids": ["M72c0a9fc78431732"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "event", "label": "Skylab 2 astronauts board astro van for Pad 39B, May 25, 1973", "media_ids": ["M5d1e7b88eb5245d1"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "context", "label": "Parasol construction by technicians in Building 10, May 23, 1973", "media_ids": ["M6e0edf21be0e2a6e"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "context", "label": "Parasol sunshade checkout at Johnson Space Center, May 23, 1973", "media_ids": ["M0dd68a44138a4e57"]}, {"type": "video", "role": "event", "label": "Skylab 2: partial spacewalk, in-orbit exercise, and undocking footage, 1973", "media_ids": ["Mf0cb4449966e0e35"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "event", "label": "Kerwin cutting strap to free the solar array, June 7, 1973", "media_ids": ["Mb0e200800b40bc79"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "context", "label": "Engineers examine tools for freeing the solar array, June 1973", "media_ids": ["Mabed1188322893a1"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "context", "label": "Ground duplicate of the strap jamming solar array wing one, June 5, 1973", "media_ids": ["Md0e3b6588bb8b7bf"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "context", "label": "Neutral Buoyancy Simulator training for Skylab repairs, May 1973", "media_ids": ["Md4b419eeec9a8a71"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "context", "label": "Illustration of Skylab with parasol partially deployed, circa 1973", "media_ids": ["M7b77c4ff300be2d8"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "context", "label": "Illustration of Skylab with parasol fully deployed, circa 1973", "media_ids": ["M8d7c74e472ada95e"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "event", "label": "Kerwin at the airlock during the June 7, 1973 spacewalk", "media_ids": ["M5aac36b1b59a7f86"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "event", "label": "Skylab with deployed parasol, photographed by departing Skylab 2 crew, 1973", "media_ids": ["M8e26ba8c3ab98a99"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "event", "label": "Skylab 2 reflected in orbit with parasol visible, 1973", "media_ids": ["Mb43810a53d9c6e5b"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "event", "label": "Skylab solar arrays exposed to the Sun, 1973", "media_ids": ["Mf7e681f0382e02a3"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "context", "label": "Conrad and Kerwin during Vestibular Function experiment training, 1973", "media_ids": ["Mda5448170ae2cd0f"]}, {"type": "video", "role": "event", "label": "Skylab 2 exercise with Pete Conrad aboard the station, 1973", "media_ids": ["M21c14cce253d3ed0"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "event", "label": "Kerwin and Conrad weightless in the Orbital Workshop, June 1, 1973", "media_ids": ["M3b595b87707f7b09"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "event", "label": "Kerwin examines Weitz during Skylab 2, June 1973", "media_ids": ["Mc87287f7297675bf"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "event", "label": "Skylab in orbit with gold sunshield as crew departs, June 22, 1973", "media_ids": ["Mbc7dc76063755a04"]}, {"type": "image", "role": "event", "label": "Skylab 2 Command Module floats after splashdown, June 22, 1973", "media_ids": ["Md7fd69851023aead"]}], "segments": [{"chapter": "A Station Wounded in Orbit", "narration": "May 1973. A quarter of a million pounds of space station climbs away from Florida. Skylab is America\'s first laboratory in orbit, built to carry three crews and years of science. But within the first minute of flight, something goes terribly wrong. By the time it reaches orbit, the station is already hurt, and the first crew is still on the ground.", "asset": 1}, {"chapter": "A Station Wounded in Orbit", "narration": "On the launch pad days earlier, two rockets stood ready. The giant Saturn V would carry the station itself. A smaller Saturn IB would carry the crew. When Skylab lifted off on May 14, 1973, it appeared to fly normally. Engineers in the control center then saw telemetry that told them damage had occurred.", "asset": 0}, {"chapter": "A Station Wounded in Orbit", "narration": "A thin micrometeoroid shield around the workshop also served as thermal protection, guarding the metal skin from the Sun. During ascent, aerodynamic forces caught it, tore it loose, and ripped it away. Debris jammed one large solar array wing. The other wing was torn off entirely.", "asset": 4}, {"chapter": "A Station Wounded in Orbit", "narration": "Without that shield, sunlight beat directly on the workshop. Internal temperatures climbed toward 126 degrees Fahrenheit. Food, film, medicines and experiments were at risk. The station\'s remaining power was limited, because one wing was gone and the other could not open. Overheated and underpowered at once.", "asset": 3}, {"chapter": "A Station Wounded in Orbit", "narration": "Even the mission\'s designers had thought the solar wings would deploy cleanly and stay out of the way. On the ground years earlier, technicians had checked a wing as it unfolded. Now, in orbit, one of those wings was pinned against the workshop by debris, its hinge frozen shut.", "asset": 2}, {"chapter": "Engineers Against the Clock", "narration": "Flight Director Donald Puddy\'s team in Houston faced two separate problems. First, cool the station. Second, restore its power. Solving one would not solve the other. Managers postponed the crew\'s launch, first by days, then by ten days in all, buying time for engineers to work.", "asset": 7}, {"chapter": "Engineers Against the Clock", "narration": "Teams at Johnson Space Center and Marshall Space Flight Center weighed three sunshade ideas. A huge twin-pole shade would require a spacewalk. A second design meant one astronaut holding a shade from an open hatch while another flew the spacecraft. Both were complex. The simplest option was a parasol deployed from inside the station.", "asset": 8}, {"chapter": "Engineers Against the Clock", "narration": "Jack Kinzler, chief of technical services at Johnson Space Center, designed it. A sheet of nylon, Mylar and aluminum, twenty-two by twenty-four feet, attached to the ends of spring-loaded fishing rods. Riggers packed it into an eight-inch square container, small enough to ride inside the Apollo spacecraft.", "asset": 6}, {"chapter": "Engineers Against the Clock", "narration": "Technicians in Building 10 assembled the parasol and tested its deployment on the ground. This was not a recording of an orbital repair. It was preparation, days before launch. Meanwhile, controllers also devised an attitude plan, turning the station to maximize power from its remaining solar observatory arrays while reducing direct solar heating.", "asset": 8}, {"chapter": "Launch and the Failed First Attempt", "narration": "The crew was Commander Pete Conrad, Pilot Paul Weitz, and Science Pilot Joe Kerwin, a physician. They trained for the repairs while engineers hurried the tools aboard. On May 25, 1973, they lifted off from Pad 39B, carrying parasols and cutting tools beneath their seats.", "asset": 4}, {"chapter": "Launch and the Failed First Attempt", "narration": "After a seven-hour chase, the Apollo spacecraft approached Skylab. Before docking, the astronauts flew around the station and inspected it carefully, describing and photographing the damage. The shield was gone. The array was jammed. Now they could see exactly what they faced.", "asset": 4}, {"chapter": "Launch and the Failed First Attempt", "narration": "Then came the first attempt to free the array. While the Apollo spacecraft held position alongside, the astronauts stood in the open hatch for a standup spacewalk. They tried, and they failed. The jammed wing refused to move. That night, the station was still overheated and still starved of power.", "asset": 10}, {"chapter": "Launch and the Failed First Attempt", "narration": "After docking, the crew entered the station. The first repair would be cooling, not power. On May 26, they pushed the folded parasol through a scientific airlock and extended it into space. The spring-loaded rods opened, the fabric spread, and the shade settled against the workshop\'s sunlit skin.", "asset": 14}, {"chapter": "The Parasol and the Second Battle", "narration": "Internal temperatures fell to comfortable levels. For the first time, the workshop felt habitable. But the parasol did not restore the missing power. With one array lost and one jammed, the station\'s electrical supply was still sharply limited. Half the rescue was complete.", "asset": 18}, {"chapter": "The Parasol and the Second Battle", "narration": "Back on Earth, the work on the array problem had continued. Backup commander Rusty Schweickart led a tiger team. Using television images and crew descriptions, engineers concluded that a metal strap, likely with a bolt, was pinning the wing against the workshop.", "asset": 12}, {"chapter": "The Parasol and the Second Battle", "narration": "In the Neutral Buoyancy Simulator at Marshall Space Flight Center, divers in spacesuits recreated the problem. Schweickart and astronaut Ed Gibson practiced techniques, using a cutting tool at the end of a long pole. Here, crews rehearsed the repair underwater, not in orbit.", "asset": 13}, {"chapter": "The Solar Array Repair", "narration": "Engineers examined the tools that might free the wing. A cable-operated cutter could clip an identical metal strap on the ground, requiring about ninety pounds of force. The same heads and extension rods would be carried into orbit. This was planning and testing, not an orbital photograph.", "asset": 11}, {"chapter": "The Solar Array Repair", "narration": "June 7, 1973. Fourteen days into the mission. All three astronauts put on spacesuits. Weitz stayed inside the docking adapter. Conrad and Kerwin depressurized the airlock. Conrad chose not to take cameras outside, prioritizing the task. The only images would come from Weitz inside the station.", "asset": 16}, {"chapter": "The Solar Array Repair", "narration": "Kerwin assembled the five sections of a twenty-five-foot pole, handing them out to Conrad. The area near the solar wing had no handrails; designers had never expected spacewalks there. The pole itself became Conrad\'s pathway down the side of the station.", "asset": 16}, {"chapter": "The Solar Array Repair", "narration": "Kerwin anchored himself and closed the cutter\'s jaws on the metal strap. It severed. The beam moved a few degrees, then stopped, its hinge frozen. Conrad and Kerwin worked their suited bodies under a rope and pushed against the beam. Suddenly, the hinge gave way. The array opened to its full ninety degrees.", "asset": 10}, {"chapter": "The Solar Array Repair", "narration": "The motion released the tension and sent both astronauts tumbling, their tethers catching them short of drifting away. Inside, Weitz and controllers watched as the three solar panels unfurled and began generating power. The repair spacewalk had lasted three hours and twenty-five minutes, the longest in Earth orbit at that time. The fix-it crew had saved Skylab.", "asset": 11}, {"chapter": "Science From a Repaired Station", "narration": "With power restored, the mission changed character. Hot coffee was possible again, and so were the experiments. The crew continued medical studies of how the body adapts to long spaceflight. Weitz and Kerwin traded places as physician and patient, measuring the human cost of a month in weightlessness.", "asset": 24}, {"chapter": "Science From a Repaired Station", "narration": "On June 15, Weitz recorded the first solar flare of the mission with the Apollo Telescope Mount instruments. The crew photographed Earth with the Earth Resources Experiment Package. In the workshop, they worked and floated in a transformed environment, once a crisis, now a working laboratory.", "asset": 19}, {"chapter": "Science From a Repaired Station", "narration": "A second spacewalk on June 19 retrieved film for the solar instruments, and Conrad brushed away debris obstructing an instrument. The first crew had surpassed the longest human spaceflight record set by the Soviet Soyuz 11 crew two years earlier. The mission was no longer a rescue. It was a success.", "asset": 21}, {"chapter": "Science From a Repaired Station", "narration": "In August 1973, after this crew was safely home, NASA prepared a rescue capability that was never needed. Two astronauts could have flown a modified Apollo spacecraft to bring back five crewmen if a spacecraft had failed. It was never used during any of Skylab\'s three missions. This was a contingency, not the repair of May and June.", "asset": 2}, {"chapter": "Return and Aftermath", "narration": "On June 22, 1973, the command module splashed down in the Pacific, about 835 miles southwest of San Diego. The prime recovery ship, USS Ticonderoga, moved in as a helicopter hovered nearby. The three astronauts had completed twenty-eight days in space, and virtually all of their planned science objectives.", "asset": 25}, {"chapter": "Return and Aftermath", "narration": "As the crew departed, the gold parasol and the freed solar wing could be seen against the station. Skylab had been repaired in flight, a feat unprecedented in spaceflight. The repairs ensured the station could support the later flights of fifty-nine and eighty-four days. The lab exceeded preflight expectations.", "asset": 25}, {"chapter": "Return and Aftermath", "narration": "Inside the workshop, Pete Conrad ran and jumped around the circular panel track, a small celebration of a repaired home. But the meaning of Skylab 2 reaches further. Lessons from its repairs informed later programs, from Salyut to the shuttle and the space station.", "asset": 20}, {"chapter": "Return and Aftermath", "narration": "This was not a story of one heroic moment. It was engineers glimpsing telemetry, technicians packing a parasol, controllers recalculating attitudes, divers rehearsing underwater, and three astronauts risking a spacewalk. Cooling the station and restoring its power were two separate challenges, answered patiently and bravely.", "asset": 10}, {"chapter": "Return and Aftermath", "narration": "Skylab was America\'s first space station, launched May 14, 1973, rescued by its first crew through a sunshade and a daring repair spacewalk. What seemed a disaster became a lesson: that a damaged vehicle, and a dedicated team, can still be made to work.", "asset": 22}, {"chapter": "Return and Aftermath", "narration": "Today, the parasol and the solar wing survive in photographs as a record of human persistence. The crew called themselves fix-it people, and they had, in two stages, preserved a laboratory and set a standard for repairing spacecraft. Their work would echo through every mission that followed.", "asset": 4}, {"chapter": "Return and Aftermath", "narration": "Skylab\'s first repair remains a testament to careful engineering and quiet courage. It showed that even when the machine breaks, and the clock is short, a team on the ground and a crew in orbit can restore the mission together.", "asset": 9}], "thumbnail": {"asset": 0, "text": "Skylab: Wounded in Orbit"}}')
